maphore for concurrency control**: Uses `asyncio.Semaphore(max_concurrent)` to limit how many tasks can run simultaneously\n- **Cleanup on interruption**: Each task runs within an `async with semaphore:` context, so when KeyboardInterrupt happens and the event loop cancels tasks, the context manager still exits cleanly. This allows `finally` blocks in your tasks' cleanup code to run.\n- **Simple API**: Just pass a list of async callables and the max concurrent limit\n\nYou can test it like this:\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom run import run_tasks\n\nasync def task_with_cleanup(n):\n    try:\n        await asyncio.sleep(10)\n        print(f\"Task {n} done\")\n    finally:\n        print(f\"Task {n} cleanup\")\n\ntasks = [lambda n=i: task_with_cleanup(n) for i in range(5)]\nawait run_tasks(tasks, max_concurrent=2)\n```\n\nIf you hit Ctrl+C, all the cleanup code will still run.","ttft_ms":11111,"type":"result","duration_ms":16776,"uuid":"96026f69-eee3-43d8-ba89-ae753945d20d","ttft_stream_ms":1073,"time_to_request_ms":75,"queued_turn_count":0}
